  "question": {
    "text": "In an experiment, a sphere of aluminium of mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is heated up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>150</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> . Immediately, it is put into water of volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>150</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cc</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>27</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> kept in a calorimeter of water equivalent to<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>025</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> .&nbsp;The final temperature of the system is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>40</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> . The specific heat of the aluminium is(take&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Joule</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>calorie</mi></math> )",
